{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003972",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches on phenotype alignment (each reports abdominal aortic aneurysm; PGS000753 is explicitly prevalent AAA, others are generic AAA). Per domain-knowledge policy, a disease-focused multi-cohort meta-analysis PRS with basic/unknown covariates should generally be preferred over pan-trait/portability framework scores when endpoints are similarly aligned. PGS003972 (Nat Genet AAA GWAS meta-analysis; many disease-focused cohorts listed) provides a coherent disease-specific evidence package and reports a full-model AUROC=0.69 in EUR evaluation (n=6,940). Competing framework-style models include PGS001784 (Global Biobank/Cell Genom; PRS-only R2=0.0147 but full-model AUROC=0.868 likely covariate-driven with extensive age interaction terms) and PGS002054 (UKB portability; only partial-r=0.0144). PGS000753 is older, very sparse (29 variants) and prevalent-only despite an OR=1.37 per SD; PGS003429 is screening/cost-effectiveness framed with lower AUROC=0.708 and unknown covariates. Selection remains sub-optimal because key PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/incremental AUROC) are missing for the disease-focused 2023 models, and PGS003973 shows a much higher full-model C-index (0.882) but is not directly comparable without PRS-only/incremental metrics and may be more covariate-driven."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003725",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are abundant (85) and many have acceptable phenotype alignment to coronary artery disease. PGS003725 is preferred because it is explicitly CAD (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both CAD), is from a disease-focused multi-ancestry PRS paper (Nat Med: “A multi-ancestry polygenic risk score improves risk prediction for coronary artery disease”), has clean/basic covariates (age, sex, ancestry PCs), large validation sample size (n=308,264), and strong reported effect sizes (HR=1.75; OR=2.14). However, confidence is not high because several competing CAD models also have strong/clean evidence and/or higher reported full-model discrimination in this context (e.g., PGS001780 AUROC=0.913 but likely covariate-driven and not PRS-only; PGS000018 AUROC=0.89 but with very small validation n=3,309), and many candidates lack directly comparable PRS-only AUC/R2 metrics, limiting definitive separation."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present (multiple hypothyroidism PRSs). PGS005218 is best supported on phenotype alignment and study archetype: it targets hypothyroidism with phenotyping_reported as incident hypothyroidism and comes from a disease-focused GWAS/PRS publication (\"Genome-wide association study and polygenic risk prediction of hypothyroidism\") with very large training (n=1,146,562) and large validation (n=441,692), unlike several other candidates that are therapy-induced thyroid dysfunction endpoints (PGS000759/761/820) or pan-trait/portability frameworks (e.g., snpnet 813 traits: PGS000965/PGS001181; UKB portability: PGS001816/PGS002024). However, the only reported AUROC for PGS005218 is a full-model AUROC=0.859 with covariates including thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the provided domain_knowledge flags as near-outcome covariates that inflate discrimination and are not comparable as PRS evidence; PRS-only metrics/effect sizes are not shown here. This limits certainty and keeps the decision at DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL despite strong endpoint/study support."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001791",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (trait_reported/trait_efo align), but PGS001791 has a cleaner endpoint statement (phenotyping_reported: “Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis” rather than incident-only) and is supported by an explicit PRS-comparable metric (PGS-only / covariates-regressed-out Nagelkerke R2 = 0.005929) plus a very large EUR validation (n=347,350). PGS004695 reports a strong HR (3.78, top vs bottom PRS quintile) in an incident IPF setting with large validation (n=402,042), but lacks PRS-comparable AUC/R2 and includes additional epidemiologic covariates (education, smoking, pack-years, BMI) that make performance less directly comparable. Confidence is not high because PGS001791 comes from a broad “Global Biobank analyses…” framework context and its only AUC shown (0.758) appears to be full-model, while PGS004695 provides only effect-size evidence (HR) without isolated PRS discrimination metrics."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001791",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001791, PGS004695) with consistent trait/efo alignment to idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. PGS001791 is preferred because it provides PRS-comparable performance evidence (PRS-only/covariates-regressed-out Nagelkerke R2=0.005929) alongside an evaluation in EUR (n=347,350) and uses only basic demographic/PC covariates, whereas PGS004695 reports only an HR (3.78 high vs low PRS quintile) with no AUC/R2 and includes a broader risk-factor covariate set (including smoking intensity and BMI), making comparability to a standalone PRS less clear. However, confidence is limited because PGS001791 comes from a global biobank framework-style paper (per title) and its reported AUROC (0.758) appears to be full-model rather than PRS-only (so cannot be used as a direct PRS discrimination metric), while PGS004695’s effect size is potentially strong but not directly comparable to PRS-only metrics."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (kidney cancer) via trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported mappings, with renal cell carcinoma noted as the clinically dominant subtype. PGS004908 is best supported on the visible evidence because it reports the strongest discrimination among the set (AUROC 0.74, 95% CI 0.72–0.75) along with a strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.5, 95% CI 1.43–1.58) and large European evaluation sample size (n=324,805), in a disease-focused GWAS meta-analysis context (Nat Genet; multi-ancestry discovery with EUR evaluation). However, this remains sub-optimal rather than high-quality because most candidates only provide full-model AUROC (not PRS-only metrics), covariate sets differ/are sometimes unknown, and the top-performing alternative family (PGS000076/PGS000787) has similarly high full-model AUROC (~0.722) with very large validation but is from cross-/pan-cancer risk-factor integration contexts, leaving residual comparability uncertainty without explicit PRS-only/incremental metrics."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002729","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to knee osteoarthritis via trait_efo/phenotyping_reported, but the evidence does not clearly identify a single high-confidence winner because most models lack PRS-only comparable performance metrics (AUC/R2) and several report only per-SD OR/HR.

PGS002729 is preferred because it has strong phenotype alignment ("Knee osteoarthritis"; phenotyping "Clinical osteoarthritis") and comes from a disease-focused publication explicitly about hip/knee OA PRS, with multi-cohort development (multiple biobanks listed) suggesting broader external orientation than UKB-only framework scores. It also provides an interpretable discrimination estimate (AUROC 0.66 with CI) in a European evaluation (n=14,926), albeit this appears to be a full-model AUROC with covariates (Age, sex, BMI), which the provided domain guidance flags as potentially non-comparable and BMI is a meaningful mediator for OA.

Key limitation: PGS002729 does not report PRS-only AUROC/R2 here, and competing models include (i) PGS001192 with explicit PRS-only metrics but from a pan-trait UKB snpnet framework and a time-to-event endpoint, and (ii) genome-wide shrinkage PRSs (e.g., PGS002767) with decent effect size (OR 1.35) but no AUC/R2 shown—making head-to-head strength hard to adjudicate from the visible metadata alone."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct-match to the target concept (nodular goiter) via either "Nontoxic multinodular goiter" (PGS001814, PGS002022) or "Benign nodular goiter" (PGS005261–PGS005263). Models PGS005273–PGS005274 are case-control contrasts involving benign nodular goiter but are not pure nodular-goiter risk scores, so they are less endpoint-faithful for the target trait.

Among the three benign nodular goiter scores, PGS005262 has the strongest visible discrimination evidence (full_model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5876 for PGS005263 and 0.5854 for PGS005261) with the same reported endpoint and the same large validation size (n=94,651) from a disease-focused multi-biobank thyroid disease study context, which domain guidance suggests should generally outrank portability-framework UKB scores when endpoints are aligned. The UKB portability models (PGS001814/PGS002022) provide only modest partial correlations (~0.024–0.028) and come from a pan-trait portability framework, making them less compelling despite having explicit PRS-comparable metrics.

Limitation: for the PGS00526x family, only full-model AUROC/effect sizes are shown and covariates are listed as Unknown, so PRS-only contribution and covariate comparability cannot be verified; the AUROC differences within the family are also small. This keeps the recommendation from being high-confidence."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000208",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 32 returned candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_efo = thyroid carcinoma / thyroid cancer). For a generic thyroid carcinoma target, models with a generic thyroid cancer endpoint are preferred over restricted survivor/secondary-cancer endpoints (e.g., PGS001354, PGS005210) or differential-diagnosis endpoints (e.g., carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter: PGS005273/PGS005274). Among the generic-endpoint candidates, the PNAS thyroid-cancer–focused family (PGS000207/208/209) provides direct disease-focused context and reports discrimination; PGS000208 has the highest reported AUROC (0.751, European validation) with a very large validation sample (n=130,279), supporting it as the best-supported direct-match option in this context. Remaining limitation: the reported AUROCs are full-model metrics with covariates that include family history (a non-comparable clinical predictor per the provided policy), and several other direct-match models exist with large validations and/or multi-ancestry development (e.g., Global Biobank PRS-CS-auto and 2026 multi-ancestry preprint PRSCS scores) but have incomplete or less directly comparable performance metadata here, so the overall evidence does not support a clearly unconflicted high-quality pick."
}

