  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000208",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to the target disease concept (thyroid carcinoma / thyroid cancer), but they vary in endpoint framing and metric comparability. Among the most endpoint-faithful generic thyroid-cancer models, the disease-focused PNAS study family (PGS000207/208/209: “Assessing thyroid cancer risk using polygenic risk scores”) provides direct thyroid cancer endpoints with reported AUROC and clear covariates; within that family, PGS000208 has the strongest reported discrimination (AUROC 0.751, European validation, n=130,279) while using only basic demographic plus family history covariates (gender, birth year, family history). Alternatives either have weaker discrimination (e.g., PGS000207 AUROC 0.692; PGS000209 AUROC 0.694), are framework/pan-trait or repository-derived with weaker metrics (e.g., Cancer PRSweb AUC ~0.52–0.64; snpnet/portability/global-biobank framework scores with modest/unclear gains), or use notably restricted survivor-only endpoints despite higher full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS001354 subsequent thyroid cancer in childhood cancer survivors). Limitation: the top metric for PGS000208 is a full-model AUROC with family history included (a non-genetic predictor), and many other candidates report only full-model AUROC/effect sizes with heterogeneous covariates, making cross-model PRS-only comparability incomplete; therefore the direct-match selection is supported but not high-certainty."
}
